---
import ArticleLayout from '../../../layouts/ArticleLayout.astro';

const path = '/blog/how-to-build-your-first-dqf/';
const title = 'How to Build Your First DQF (Driver Qualification File)';
const description =
  'A step-by-step guide to building a driver qualification file: which documents belong in it, how long each one lasts, common gaps, and how to track the next due date.';
---

<ArticleLayout title={title} description={description} path={path}>
  <p>
    A driver qualification file (DQF) is the record a motor carrier keeps for each driver it employs
    or uses. It is not a single form. It is a folder of documents that prove the driver was
    qualified when hired and has stayed qualified since. For a new owner-operator, the first DQF is
    usually assembled in a hurry after authority is granted, and the dates are entered once and then
    forgotten.
  </p>
  <p>
    This guide walks through the documents, the dates that matter, and the places where a small
    fleet most often loses track. It is a guide, not a compliance determination. The rule references
    are engineering pointers, not legal advice.
  </p>

  <h2>What belongs in a driver qualification file</h2>
  <p>
    Under 49 CFR Part 391, a DQF generally includes the driver's application, a motor vehicle record
    (MVR) from each state where the driver held a license in the past three years, a road test
    certificate or equivalent, a medical examiner's certificate, employment verification, and the
    annual review of the driving record. Some documents are collected once. Others repeat on a
    schedule.
  </p>
  <p>
    For a small operation, it helps to separate the folder into two ideas: <strong>one-time
    documents</strong> that prove the driver was qualified at hire, and <strong>recurring
    documents</strong> that need a new date on a regular cadence. A folder that mixes the two tends
    to look complete while a recurring date quietly passes.
  </p>

  <h2>The one-time documents</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Road test certificate.</strong> 49 CFR 391.31. The road test is a one-time document
      unless the driver changes employers or the certificate is no longer valid under the rule.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Employment verification.</strong> 49 CFR 391.51(b)(2). The file should show that the
      previous employer's safety performance history was requested and reviewed. Like the road test,
      it is a hire-time document, not a recurring date.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Driver application and related hiring records.</strong> These are also one-time, but
      they are not date calculations. Keep them in the folder and review them when a driver moves to
      a new role.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    One-time does not mean "file it and forget it." If a driver leaves and is rehired, or if the
    operation changes from non-CDL to CDL, the one-time documents may need to be refreshed. The
    tracker shows them as "on file" instead of inventing a due date.
  </p>

  <h2>The recurring documents and their dates</h2>
  <p>
    These are the dates that move. Each one has an anchor date you already know and a period you can
    calculate.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Medical examiner's certificate.</strong> 49 CFR 391.45(b). The certificate itself
      carries an expiration date, and that date always wins. If a driver only remembers the issue
      date, a 24-month estimate is a fallback, not a guarantee. Examiners can issue a shorter
      certificate.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>MVR pull.</strong> 49 CFR 391.25(c)(2). The next pull is generally due 12 months after
      the last one. The date you enter should be the date the report was actually pulled, not the
      date it was filed.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Annual review of driving record.</strong> 49 CFR 391.25(a). The review is an annual
      event. Twelve months after the last review is a useful planning date, even if your internal
      process runs a few days early.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>CDL expiration.</strong> 49 CFR Part 383. CDL validity is set by the issuing state.
      There is no single federal cycle to assume, so the tracker uses only the expiration date on
      the license.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>A simple build order</h2>
  <ol>
    <li>
      <strong>Start with the driver, not the folder.</strong> Create one record per driver. Use a
      name or a nickname; the label is only for you.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Enter the dates you can prove.</strong> Pull the medical certificate, the last MVR,
      the license, and the last annual review. Enter the dates printed on those documents. If a date
      is missing, leave it blank rather than guessing.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Mark one-time documents as on file.</strong> Add the road test and employment
      verification dates so the folder has a complete shape. The tracker will not turn them into
      recurring due dates.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Write down the next date for each recurring item.</strong> The tracker calculates
      medical, MVR, CDL, and annual review dates and groups them into overdue, 30-day, and 60-day
      buckets.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Back up the file.</strong> Export a JSON backup before clearing browser data or moving
      to another device. The export contains the labels and dates you entered, so store it somewhere
      private.
    </li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Try it with example dates</h2>
  <p>
    You can see the calculation without typing anything by opening the tracker with a set of example
    dates. The link below fills a draft driver with a medical expiration, an MVR pull, an annual
    review, a road test, and an employment verification date. The draft is not saved until you click
    "Save these dates."
  </p>
  <p>
    <a href="/?medical=2026-05-01&amp;mvr=2026-10-01&amp;annual=2026-08-15&amp;road=2024-02-01&amp;ev=2024-01-15" data-cta-source="article_cta">
      Open the DQF expiration tracker with these example dates
    </a>
  </p>
  <p>
    The example uses a medical expiration in the past so you can see the overdue state. Change any
    date to match your own records, or clear the example and start with a blank driver.
  </p>

  <h2>Common gaps in a first DQF</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Using the issue date as the expiration date.</strong> The certificate's printed
      expiration date is the one that matters. The issue date is only a fallback for an estimate.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Recording the MVR file date instead of the pull date.</strong> The 12-month clock
      starts when the report is pulled, not when it is filed.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Assuming a CDL lasts a fixed number of years.</strong> State-issued credentials vary.
      Enter the date on the license.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Skipping the annual review because the MVR is current.</strong> They are separate
      requirements with separate dates.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Keeping the only copy in one browser.</strong> A cleared browser profile removes
      localStorage. Export a backup and keep it with the rest of your records.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2>What the tracker does not do</h2>
  <p>
    The tracker does not decide whether your file is complete, and it does not replace a safety
    consultant, an audit, or legal advice. It covers six date-based documents and compares them with
    your device date. Random testing is a program-level requirement and is listed for reference
    only. If a rule changes, the rule version on the page tells you when the table was last updated.
  </p>
  <p>
    The value is narrow but real: the next date is visible before it becomes a problem. For a
    one-person operation, that is often the difference between a calm file review and a rushed
    search for a document that should have been refreshed months ago.
  </p>

  <h2>Keep the next date in front of you</h2>
  <p>
    Once the first driver is in, add the rest of the fleet up to five drivers. The fleet summary
    shows the three most urgent items across all drivers, so you do not have to open each file to
    find the next problem. When you are ready to start, open the tracker with a blank file or with
    the example dates above.
  </p>
  <p>
    <a href="/?medical=2027-01-15&amp;mvr=2026-11-01&amp;cdl=2028-03-15" data-cta-source="article_cta">
      Start tracking your own DQF dates
    </a>
  </p>
  <p class="note">
    Estimated based on FMCSA Part 391. Not legal advice. This is a guide, not a compliance
